          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the majority of the three games being played simultaneously.',
          gameplay: "Play follows standard Gin Rummy: each turn a player draws one card from the stock or the top of the discard pile, then discards one card (if drawing from discard, must discard a different card). Players form melds (sets of equal rank or runs in the same suit) and reduce deadwood. A player may knock when deadwood is 10 or less by discarding face down and exposing their hand; the opponent may lay off cards on the knocker's melds unless the knocker went gin. Scoring: knocker scores the difference in deadwood, or the opponent scores the difference plus 10 for an undercut; going gin scores 20 plus the opponent's deadwood. Hollywood scoring: three games are tracked on one score sheet. The first hand won is recorded in Game 1 only; the second win in Games 1 and 2; the third and all subsequent wins in all three games. Each game runs to 100 points then receives game and box bonuses; when all three games are finished the totals are summed for the match score.",
          keyRules: [
            'Follow the legal action constraints defined for Hollywood Gin.',
            'Round outcome is resolved by the scoring method: Scoring summary: Scoring follows the game-specific point or pot rules.',
            'Winning condition: Win the majority of the three games being played simultaneously.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Winning the first hand is extremely valuable as it 'opens' your first column and gets you closer to opening the second and third.",
          intermediate: "If you have a large lead in Game 1 but are trailing in Games 2 and 3, you may play more conservatively to 'lock in' the first game win while trying to gain ground in the others.",
          advanced: "Managing the 'knock' timing across multiple game states. If your opponent is close to 100 in Game 1, you might take higher risks to prevent them from taking that specific game bonus.",
          tips: [
            "A 'Slam' (winning every hand in a game) is much harder in Hollywood Gin but results in massive total match scores.",
            "The 'Undercut' bonus is especially impactful when it applies across three columns.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
